

# 0. MANDATORY AI DESIGN SKILL GOVERNANCE — UI UX PRO MAX

## 0.1 Status

**MANDATORY. NON-OPTIONAL.**

All UI/UX design and frontend implementation work for this project MUST use:

**UI UX Pro Max Skill**  
Repository: https://github.com/nextlevelbuilder/ui-ux-pro-max-skill

The coding agent MUST NOT independently invent a visual system before consulting and activating the installed UI UX Pro Max skill.

The skill supports AI coding assistants including Codex CLI and provides an automatic skill mode for UI/UX work, as well as design-system generation, product/style/color/typography recommendations, and pre-delivery anti-pattern checks.

---

## 0.2 Installation Verification

Before implementing any user interface, the coding agent MUST verify that the UI UX Pro Max skill is available for the current AI coding environment.

For Codex CLI, the project setup should use the platform supported by the UI UX Pro Max CLI.

Recommended installation workflow:

```bash
npm install -g uipro-cli
uipro init --ai codex
```

If the current environment already has the skill installed, do NOT reinstall it unnecessarily.

If the skill is missing:

1. Install or initialize the skill for the current AI assistant.
2. Confirm that the generated skill files are available to the coding agent.
3. Restart or reload the coding assistant when required by the installation workflow.
4. Only then begin UI implementation.

The coding agent MUST NOT silently skip this requirement.

---

## 0.3 Mandatory Execution Order

The following execution order is required:

```text
READ PRD
    ↓
VERIFY UI UX PRO MAX SKILL
    ↓
ACTIVATE / INVOKE SKILL
    ↓
ANALYZE PRODUCT TYPE
    ↓
GENERATE PROJECT DESIGN SYSTEM
    ↓
RECORD DESIGN DECISIONS
    ↓
IMPLEMENT FOUNDATION
    ↓
IMPLEMENT PAGES
    ↓
RUN RESPONSIVE REVIEW
    ↓
RUN ACCESSIBILITY REVIEW
    ↓
RUN UI/UX ANTI-PATTERN CHECK
    ↓
FIX ALL HIGH-PRIORITY ISSUES
    ↓
RUN BUILD / TYPE / LINT VALIDATION
    ↓
FINAL DELIVERY
```

No page should be considered complete before the applicable UI UX Pro Max review process is performed.

---

## 0.4 Product Classification for Design Reasoning

When using UI UX Pro Max, the agent MUST classify this project using the closest applicable combination of:

- Technical service platform
- Service marketplace
- E-commerce-style service discovery
- Technology support
- Android software services

The agent MUST NOT classify the product purely as:

- Generic SaaS
- Generic crypto
- Generic hacker tool
- Generic e-commerce store

The visual recommendations must be based on the actual product:

> Marketplace-inspired Android technical service platform focused on service discovery and human consultation.

---

## 0.5 Design System Generation Is Mandatory

Before page implementation, the coding agent MUST use UI UX Pro Max to generate a project-specific design system.

The design system generation must evaluate, at minimum:

1. Product / industry fit
2. Layout pattern
3. UI style
4. Color palette
5. Typography pairing
6. Interaction effects
7. Accessibility requirements
8. Responsive requirements
9. Anti-patterns to avoid
10. Pre-delivery validation criteria

The output MUST become the project's UI source of truth.

---

## 0.6 Design System Source of Truth

Create and maintain a project-level design system document.

Recommended location:

```text
design-system/MASTER.md
```

The document MUST contain the final decisions for:

### Brand and Product Context

- Product name
- Product category
- Target audience
- Product positioning
- Design personality

### Visual System

- Primary color
- Secondary color
- Accent / CTA color
- Background colors
- Surface colors
- Text colors
- Semantic warning colors
- Border colors

### Typography

- Heading font
- Body font
- Font weights
- Heading scale
- Body scale
- Line-height rules

### Spacing

- Base spacing unit
- Spacing scale
- Section spacing
- Component spacing

### Shape

- Border radius scale
- Border rules
- Shadow rules

### Components

- Buttons
- Inputs
- Cards
- Badges
- Navigation
- Accordion
- Search
- CTA sections

### Interaction

- Hover behavior
- Focus behavior
- Active behavior
- Transition rules
- Reduced-motion behavior

### Accessibility

- Contrast requirements
- Focus requirements
- Keyboard requirements
- Touch target requirements

### Explicit Anti-Patterns

The design system MUST list patterns that this project must avoid.

---

## 0.7 Page-Specific Overrides

The global design system is the default.

If a page requires a justified exception, create a page-specific design override document rather than silently changing global styles.

Recommended structure:

```text
design-system/
├── MASTER.md
└── pages/
    ├── home.md
    ├── services.md
    ├── service-detail.md
    ├── remote-guide.md
    └── faq.md
```

A page override MAY define:

- Different content density
- Different section composition
- Context-specific CTA placement
- Context-specific illustration treatment

A page override MUST NOT arbitrarily change:

- Brand identity
- Core typography
- Core color semantics
- Global component language

unless explicitly approved as a documented design exception.

---

## 0.8 Mandatory UI UX Pro Max Consultation Before Each Major UI Phase

The coding agent MUST consult the UI UX Pro Max skill before implementing:

### Phase A — App Shell

- Header
- Mobile navigation
- Footer
- Global buttons
- Global typography

### Phase B — Homepage

- Hero
- Search
- Category discovery
- Featured services
- Conversion sections

### Phase C — Service Discovery

- Listing page
- Filters
- Search results
- Service cards

### Phase D — Service Detail

- Content hierarchy
- Requirements
- Process timeline
- Important notices
- Consultation CTA

### Phase E — Remote Guide

- Step-based instructional UX
- Download card
- Preparation checklist
- Security notice

### Phase F — Final Audit

- Anti-pattern check
- Responsive review
- Accessibility review

---

## 0.9 Prohibited Agent Behavior

The coding agent MUST NOT:

1. Generate a generic AI-looking landing page.
2. Use random gradients as a substitute for visual identity.
3. Apply purple/pink "AI aesthetic" by default.
4. Use emojis as the primary icon system.
5. Create a cyberpunk or hacker aesthetic unless specifically supported by the generated design system.
6. Clone Shopee, Lazada, or another company's UI.
7. Use inconsistent spacing from page to page.
8. Invent a new color palette per page.
9. Use excessive glassmorphism.
10. Put every content block inside a card.
11. Use excessive rounded containers.
12. Use fake social proof.
13. Add animations without functional purpose.
14. Ignore reduced-motion preferences.
15. Skip design-system generation because the agent believes it already knows good UI.

---

## 0.10 Mandatory Icon Policy

Use a professional SVG icon library compatible with the selected stack.

Preferred behavior:

- Use semantic icons.
- Maintain consistent stroke and visual weight.
- Pair icons with labels when clarity requires it.
- Do not use emoji characters as production UI icons.

---

## 0.11 Mandatory Responsive Validation

Before final delivery, validate the UI at minimum around:

```text
375px
768px
1024px
1440px
```

For each major page, verify:

- No horizontal overflow
- Text does not clip
- Chips and badges wrap correctly
- Navigation remains usable
- CTA buttons remain reachable
- Cards reflow correctly
- Search remains usable
- Important content is not hidden

---

## 0.12 Mandatory Accessibility Validation

Before final delivery, verify:

- Meaningful color contrast
- Visible keyboard focus
- Semantic headings
- Accessible buttons and links
- Form labels
- Keyboard navigation
- Accessible accordion states
- Reduced-motion support where animations exist

---

## 0.13 Mandatory UI Anti-Pattern Gate

The implementation MUST pass a final anti-pattern review informed by UI UX Pro Max.

The final review must explicitly check for:

### Visual

- Excessive gradients
- Excessive glass effects
- Random color usage
- Inconsistent radius
- Excessive shadows
- Excessive cards
- Generic AI aesthetic

### Layout

- Weak visual hierarchy
- Unclear primary CTA
- Overcrowded sections
- Inconsistent spacing
- Poor mobile reflow

### Interaction

- Clickable-looking non-interactive elements
- Hidden actions
- Missing focus states
- Hover-only functionality

### Content

- Fake reviews
- Fake ratings
- Fake sales numbers
- Unsupported guarantees
- Lorem ipsum
- Dead CTA

Any high-priority issue MUST be fixed before delivery.

---

## 0.14 Required Agent Completion Report

When implementation is complete, the coding agent MUST provide a concise implementation report containing:

1. UI UX Pro Max installation/activation status.
2. Design system files created.
3. Main product classification used.
4. Major UI decisions.
5. Pages implemented.
6. Responsive viewports checked.
7. Accessibility checks completed.
8. Anti-pattern issues found and fixed.
9. Build status.
10. Lint/type-check status.
11. Remaining known limitations.

Do not claim a check was performed unless it was actually performed.

---

# END OF MANDATORY UI UX PRO MAX GOVERNANCE


# PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT (PRD) — VERSION 2
## Frontend Platform — Marketplace-Inspired Android Technical Services

**Document Status:** Draft / Implementation Ready  
**Version:** 2.0  
**Date:** 2026-09-08  
**Product Type:** Frontend-first service discovery platform  
**Primary Stack:** Next.js + TypeScript  
**Primary Objective:** Convert qualified visitors into WhatsApp or Telegram consultations

---

# 1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY

## 1.1 Product Overview

Build a professional, high-trust, marketplace-inspired web platform for Android software and device technical services.

The platform will initially operate as a single-provider service business. The owner and internal team provide all services. The information architecture and data model must, however, be designed so the product can later evolve into a multi-technician marketplace without requiring a full frontend rewrite.

The platform must make it easy for users to:

1. Discover services.
2. Search for a technical problem or service.
3. Understand whether a service is relevant to their device issue.
4. Read requirements, preparation steps, limitations, and risk information.
5. Contact Customer Service through WhatsApp or Telegram.
6. Consult with a human CS representative.
7. Agree on the service and execution method.
8. Follow the remote-session preparation guide.
9. Continue to the operational process outside the website.

The initial product is intentionally **frontend-first**. It must not simulate backend functionality that does not exist.

---

## 1.2 Core Business Model

### Phase 1 — Single Provider

The platform is a service catalog and conversion system.

```text
Visitor
  ↓
Website
  ↓
Search / Browse
  ↓
Service Detail
  ↓
WhatsApp or Telegram Consultation
  ↓
Human CS Qualification
  ↓
Agreement / Deal
  ↓
Remote Preparation
  ↓
Service Execution
```

### Phase 2 — Transaction Platform

Potential additions:

- Customer accounts
- Orders
- Payments
- Order status
- Service history
- Customer notifications

### Phase 3 — Multi-Technician Marketplace

Potential additions:

- Technician onboarding
- Technician profiles
- Ratings and reviews
- Service ownership
- Marketplace commission
- Technician availability
- Technician selection

---

## 1.3 Product Positioning

The product must NOT be designed as a clone of Shopee, Lazada, or another marketplace.

The intended positioning is:

> A professional Android technical service platform with marketplace-style discovery.

The experience combines:

- Marketplace discoverability
- Professional service presentation
- Technical clarity
- Human consultation
- Remote-service preparation

---

# 2. BUSINESS PROBLEM

Users with Android software problems frequently encounter three problems:

1. They do not know what service they need.
2. They do not understand technical terminology.
3. They do not know whether their specific device or issue can be handled.

A generic service landing page is insufficient because users need to explore multiple categories and compare possible solutions.

A traditional e-commerce checkout is also unsuitable for the initial business model because:

- Device conditions vary.
- The exact issue may require qualification.
- Compatibility must be checked.
- Pricing may depend on the device and complexity.
- Human consultation occurs before execution.

The website must therefore optimize for:

> **Problem discovery → understanding → trust → consultation**

instead of forcing users directly into checkout.

---

# 3. PRODUCT GOALS

## 3.1 Primary Goals

1. Present technical services in a way understandable to non-technical users.
2. Increase qualified consultations through WhatsApp and Telegram.
3. Create a professional and trustworthy brand presence.
4. Provide marketplace-like service discovery.
5. Support SEO acquisition for service and problem-related searches.
6. Make the remote-service process understandable before the customer contacts CS.
7. Establish a scalable information architecture for future marketplace expansion.

## 3.2 Secondary Goals

- Reduce repetitive pre-sales questions.
- Improve service discovery.
- Improve lead quality.
- Provide reusable service content architecture.
- Prepare a component system suitable for future expansion.

## 3.3 Non-Goals for MVP

Do NOT implement:

- User authentication
- Customer accounts
- Real-time chat
- Payment processing
- Order management
- Technician dashboard
- Admin dashboard
- Database persistence
- Real-time availability
- Fake checkout
- Fake ratings
- Fake order counters
- Fake testimonials

The product must never imply functionality that does not exist.

---

# 4. TARGET USERS

## 4.1 Persona A — General Android User

Characteristics:

- Limited technical knowledge.
- Uses common terminology such as "HP stuck logo".
- May not know the difference between bootloop, brick, firmware, or recovery.
- Needs clear instructions and human assistance.

Primary needs:

- Understand the problem.
- Find the relevant service.
- Contact someone quickly.

---

## 4.2 Persona B — Advanced Android User

Characteristics:

- Familiar with root, bootloader, ROM, recovery, and system modification.
- Wants detailed service requirements.
- May search for specific device or service terms.

Primary needs:

- Clear compatibility information.
- Detailed requirements.
- Direct technical consultation.

---

## 4.3 Persona C — Urgent Problem User

Characteristics:

- Device is not functioning correctly.
- May be stressed or unable to interpret technical information.
- Needs a clear next action.

Primary needs:

- Fast problem identification.
- Clear CTA.
- Immediate path to consultation.

---

# 5. SERVICE CATALOG

The initial information architecture must support at least the following top-level categories.

## 5.1 Root Android

Possible service entries:

- Root Android
- Device-specific root support
- Magisk-related root assistance

---

## 5.2 Unlock Bootloader

Possible service entries:

- Bootloader unlock assistance
- Device-specific unlock support

---

## 5.3 Fix Bootloop

Possible issue types:

- Stuck at boot logo
- Restart loop
- Failed system boot
- Repeated boot failure

---

## 5.4 Unbrick

Possible service scope:

- Soft brick assessment
- Software recovery
- Failed software modification recovery

---

## 5.5 Flash Firmware

Possible service scope:

- Stock firmware installation
- Firmware restoration
- Supported upgrade or downgrade assistance

---

## 5.6 Custom ROM

Possible service scope:

- Custom ROM installation assistance
- ROM migration
- Supported post-installation troubleshooting

---

## 5.7 Recovery

Possible service scope:

- Recovery-related setup
- Supported recovery troubleshooting

---

## 5.8 Software Repair

General category for:

- Software errors
- Failed updates
- System instability
- Other supported software troubleshooting

---

# 6. PRODUCT PRINCIPLES

## 6.1 Human Before Automation

The website guides users toward human consultation.

Do not design the MVP as if every issue can be automatically diagnosed.

## 6.2 Honest Capability

Never guarantee:

- 100% success
- Zero risk
- Universal compatibility
- Guaranteed data preservation

Service suitability must be confirmed during consultation.

## 6.3 Clear Before Clever

Avoid technical visual gimmicks that reduce comprehension.

The interface should prioritize:

- Clear hierarchy
- Obvious actions
- Plain-language explanations
- Consistent components

## 6.4 Marketplace Discovery, Not Marketplace Cloning

Use marketplace patterns where useful:

- Search
- Categories
- Service cards
- Browsing
- Featured services
- Filters

Do not copy another platform's branding or UI identity.

## 6.5 No Fake Product Signals

Do not display fabricated:

- Reviews
- Ratings
- Customer counts
- "X people viewing"
- Sales counts
- Scarcity timers

---

# 7. PRIMARY USER JOURNEY

## 7.1 Standard Discovery Flow

```text
Homepage
  ↓
Search or Category
  ↓
Service Listing
  ↓
Service Detail
  ↓
Read Requirements / Process
  ↓
Contact CS
  ↓
Human Consultation
```

## 7.2 Problem-First Flow

```text
Search "HP bootloop"
  ↓
Relevant Service Result
  ↓
Fix Bootloop Detail
  ↓
Explain Device Condition
  ↓
WhatsApp / Telegram
```

## 7.3 Remote Preparation Flow

```text
Consultation Complete
  ↓
Customer Receives Instructions
  ↓
Open Remote Guide
  ↓
Prepare PC / Laptop
  ↓
Prepare USB Cable
  ↓
Prepare Stable Internet
  ↓
Download AnyDesk
  ↓
Wait for CS / Technician Instructions
```

---

# 8. INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE

## 8.1 Required Routes

```text
/
 /services
 /services/[slug]
 /categories/[slug]
 /how-it-works
 /remote-guide
 /faq
 /about
