```

Optional future routes must not be implemented as fake screens.

Future-only examples:

```text
/account
/orders
/technicians
/dashboard
```

---

# 9. NAVIGATION REQUIREMENTS

## 9.1 Desktop Header

Required items:

- Brand logo
- Home
- Services
- Categories
- How It Works
- Remote Guide
- FAQ
- Search
- WhatsApp CTA
- Telegram CTA

The navigation must remain visually simple.

Do not overload the header.

---

## 9.2 Mobile Navigation

Requirements:

- Mobile-first usability.
- Search accessible immediately.
- Important consultation CTA easy to access.
- Menu items grouped logically.
- No tiny touch targets.

Recommended behavior:

- Compact top header
- Search entry point
- Mobile navigation drawer or sheet
- Persistent but non-obstructive consultation CTA

---

# 10. HOMEPAGE REQUIREMENTS

## 10.1 Homepage Objective

The homepage must answer four questions within the first screenful:

1. What is this website?
2. What services are available?
3. What should I do if I have a problem?
4. How do I contact support?

---

## 10.2 Section Order

Recommended homepage structure:

1. Announcement / trust bar
2. Header
3. Hero
4. Search
5. Service categories
6. Featured services
7. Problem discovery or quick-help section
8. How it works
9. Remote service preparation preview
10. Why choose the service
11. FAQ
12. Final consultation CTA
13. Footer

---

## 10.3 Hero Requirements

Hero content must include:

- Clear value proposition
- Short supporting text
- Primary consultation CTA
- Secondary browse-services CTA

Example content direction:

Headline:

> Butuh Bantuan untuk Software Android?

Supporting text:

> Temukan layanan untuk root, bootloader, bootloop, firmware, custom ROM, dan berbagai kendala software perangkat.

Primary CTA:

> Konsultasi Sekarang

Secondary CTA:

> Lihat Semua Layanan

The exact copy may be refined during implementation.

---

## 10.4 Search

Search is a core product feature.

Search input must support:

- Service names
- Problem terms
- Partial keywords
- Category keywords

Example queries:

- root
- bootloop
- HP stuck logo
- flash firmware
- custom ROM

Requirements:

- Case insensitive
- Fast client-side filtering for MVP
- Empty state
- Accessible keyboard interaction
- Clear search result labels

Do not implement artificial search functionality.

---

## 10.5 Service Categories

Required categories:

- Root Android
- Unlock Bootloader
- Fix Bootloop
- Unbrick
- Flash Firmware
- Custom ROM
- Recovery
- Software Repair

Desktop:

- Responsive multi-column grid.

Mobile:

- Two-column or optimized vertical layout depending on available width.

Each category card must:

- Have a recognizable icon.
- Include a concise title.
- Be clickable.
- Lead to a relevant category or filtered listing.

---

## 10.6 Featured Services

Requirements:

- Display selected services from static data.
- Do not display fabricated rating data.
- Use consistent cards.
- Make the primary next action obvious.

Recommended service card content:

- Service icon or visual
- Service name
- Short description
- Relevant support badges
- Detail CTA

Optional badge examples:

- Remote support
- Consultation required

---

# 11. SERVICE LISTING PAGE

## Route

```text
/services
```

## Requirements

The page must support:

- Search
- Category filtering
- Device brand filtering only if reliable static data exists
- Clear results count only when calculated from actual data
- Empty state
- Responsive service grid

Do not expose filters that cannot produce meaningful results.

---

# 12. SERVICE DETAIL PAGE

## Route

```text
/services/[slug]
```

Each service detail page must be generated from structured service data.

## Required Sections

### 12.1 Breadcrumb

Example:

```text
Home / Services / Fix Bootloop
```

### 12.2 Service Hero

Must include:

- Service name
- Concise explanation
- Service category
- Consultation CTA

### 12.3 Overview

Explain:

- What the service is.
- What type of problem it addresses.
- Who it may be suitable for.

### 12.4 Possible Issues / Use Cases

Use bullet points.

### 12.5 Customer Preparation

Examples where relevant:

- PC or laptop
- USB cable
- Stable internet
- Adequate battery
- Backup of important data when possible

### 12.6 Process

Explain the real process:

1. Contact CS.
2. Explain the device issue.
3. CS performs initial qualification.
4. Feasibility and process are discussed.
5. Customer and CS reach agreement.
6. Customer prepares for execution.
7. Remote guidance is followed when applicable.

### 12.7 Important Notice

Display risks honestly.

Example topics:

- Software modification may carry risk.
- Data loss may be possible depending on the condition and process.
- Device compatibility must be evaluated.
- Outcomes cannot be guaranteed before assessment.

### 12.8 Final CTA

Provide:

- WhatsApp consultation
- Telegram consultation

---

# 13. CONTACT AND CONSULTATION SYSTEM

The MVP does not include internal chat.

## 13.1 WhatsApp

The CTA must open the configured WhatsApp contact destination.

The message should include contextual information when possible.

Template concept:

```text
Halo CS, saya ingin konsultasi.

Layanan: {serviceName}

Device Brand:
Device Model:
Android / OS Version (jika diketahui):
Masalah yang Dialami:

Terima kasih.
```

The exact phone number must be configured through environment variables or centralized configuration.

Never hardcode the production contact number across multiple components.

Recommended:

```text
NEXT_PUBLIC_WHATSAPP_NUMBER
```

---

## 13.2 Telegram

Telegram CTA must be configurable.

Recommended configuration:

```text
NEXT_PUBLIC_TELEGRAM_URL
```

Do not hardcode contact destinations in multiple files.

---

# 14. REMOTE GUIDE REQUIREMENTS

## Route

```text
/remote-guide
```

## 14.1 Purpose

Prepare the customer before a remote support session.

The page must be useful even when opened from WhatsApp or Telegram.

---

## 14.2 Required Sections

### Before You Begin

Checklist:

- PC or laptop available
- Stable internet
- Suitable USB cable if required
- Device sufficiently charged
- Follow CS instructions

### Download Remote Software

Provide the official AnyDesk download destination:

- Windows
- Android where applicable

The implementation must use the official AnyDesk website as the outbound destination.

The actual outbound URLs should be stored in centralized configuration.

### Installation / Launch

Explain:

1. Download the official application.
2. Open the application.
3. Follow the CS instructions.
4. Do not grant access to unknown parties.

### Security Notice

This is mandatory.

The website must clearly state:

- Do not provide remote access to unknown individuals.
- Only continue after confirming the official technician or CS.
- Follow the communication channel used for the consultation.
- Do not share sensitive information unnecessarily.

Do not request passwords, banking information, or unrelated sensitive data through the website.

---

# 15. HOW IT WORKS PAGE

## Route

```text
/how-it-works
```

Required steps:

1. Find a service.
2. Read the service information.
3. Contact Customer Service.
4. Explain the device condition.
5. Receive initial guidance.
6. Agree on the next process.
7. Prepare for remote support if applicable.
8. Continue with the service process.

The page should also explain that service feasibility depends on the specific device condition.

---

# 16. FAQ REQUIREMENTS

## Route

```text
/faq
```

Initial FAQ topics:

- How do I choose the right service?
- Can every Android device be supported?
- Is the process performed remotely?
- What should I prepare before remote support?
- Can data be affected?
- How do I contact CS?
- When is AnyDesk required?
- What happens after consultation?

Use accordion interaction with accessible controls.

---

# 17. SEARCH REQUIREMENTS

## 17.1 MVP Search Architecture

Use static structured data.

Example sources:

```text
data/services.ts
data/categories.ts
data/faq.ts
```

The search logic may initially run client-side.

The architecture should allow migration to server-side or search infrastructure later.

---

## 17.2 Search Result Types

Results may include:

- Services
- Categories
- FAQ entries

Do not mix result types without clear labels.

Example:

```text
Services
  Fix Bootloop

Categories
  Software Repair

Help
  What should I prepare before remote support?
```

---

# 18. DESIGN SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS

The implementation should follow a deliberate design system rather than generating each page independently.

The design direction must be informed by the referenced UI/UX quality guidelines from the provided repository, including:

- Consistent hierarchy
- Appropriate typography
- Responsive behavior
- Accessibility
- Product-appropriate visual style
- Avoidance of common UI anti-patterns

The repository is a design-quality reference, not a requirement to copy another application's visual output.

---

## 18.1 Design Personality

Required qualities:

- Professional
- Modern
- Technical
- Trustworthy
- Clean
- Approachable
- Fast

Avoid making the website look like:

- A generic AI landing page
- A crypto website
- A hacker terminal
- A cyberpunk poster
- A Shopee clone

---

## 18.2 Typography

Use a modern, highly legible sans-serif stack.

Requirements:

- Strong heading hierarchy
- Comfortable body line-height
- Readable mobile text
- No decorative font as primary UI font

---

## 18.3 Spacing

Use a consistent spacing scale.

Do not apply arbitrary spacing values page by page.

Example scale may use multiples of 4.

---

## 18.4 Border Radius

Use a limited, consistent radius scale.

Do not mix:

- Fully pill-shaped cards
- Sharp cards
- Extremely rounded cards

without a defined component purpose.

---

## 18.5 Color

The final brand palette may be configured later.

Until a brand palette is finalized:

- Use a restrained professional base.
- Ensure accessible contrast.
- Use semantic colors for warning and status.
- Do not use excessive gradients.

---

## 18.6 Cards

Cards should communicate grouping.

Do not put every text block inside a card.

Avoid:

- Card inside card inside card
- Excessive shadows
- Excessive borders
- Excessive badges

---

# 19. RESPONSIVE REQUIREMENTS

## 19.1 Mobile First

Mobile must be treated as a primary experience.

Requirements:

- No horizontal scrolling.
- Minimum comfortable touch target sizes.
- Readable text without zooming.
- Search accessible near the top.
- Consultation CTA easy to reach.
- Grids collapse appropriately.

---

## 19.2 Breakpoints

Implementation may use project-standard breakpoints, but layouts must be tested at minimum for:

- Small mobile
- Standard mobile
- Tablet
- Laptop
- Wide desktop

Do not optimize only for a single viewport width.

---

# 20. ACCESSIBILITY REQUIREMENTS

Minimum requirements:

- Semantic HTML
- Proper heading hierarchy
- Visible keyboard focus
- Accessible buttons and links
- Labels for form controls
- Sufficient color contrast
- Alt text for meaningful images
- Decorative images ignored by screen readers where appropriate
- No interaction that depends only on hover

FAQ accordions must expose accessible expanded/collapsed states.

Mobile navigation must be keyboard accessible where relevant.

---

# 21. SEO REQUIREMENTS

SEO is a first-class requirement.

## 21.1 Metadata

Every important route must provide:

- Unique title
- Unique meta description
- Canonical URL when configured
- Open Graph metadata
- Social sharing metadata

---

## 21.2 Structured Data

Use only structured data that matches actual page content.

Potential schemas:

- Organization
- WebSite
- Service
- FAQPage
- BreadcrumbList

Do not generate misleading structured data.

---

## 21.3 Technical SEO

Required:

- sitemap
- robots configuration
- semantic HTML
- crawlable service pages
- stable route structure

Avoid hiding primary service content behind client-only interactions when server rendering is appropriate.

---

## 21.4 Content SEO

Service pages should target real search intent.

Examples:

- Fix Bootloop Android
- Jasa Root Android
- Flash Firmware Android
- Custom ROM Assistance

Do not keyword-stuff.

---

# 22. NEXT.JS TECHNICAL REQUIREMENTS

## 22.1 Recommended Stack

- Next.js
- TypeScript
- React
- Tailwind CSS
- ESLint
- Component-based architecture

Use the current stable project conventions appropriate to the environment.

---

## 22.2 Application Architecture

Recommended structure:

```text
src/
├── app/
│   ├── page.tsx
│   ├── layout.tsx
│   ├── services/
│   │   ├── page.tsx
│   │   └── [slug]/
│   │       └── page.tsx
│   ├── categories/
│   │   └── [slug]/
│   ├── how-it-works/
│   ├── remote-guide/
│   ├── faq/
│   ├── about/
│   ├── contact/
│   ├── disclaimer/
│   ├── privacy/
│   ├── terms/
│   └── search/
│
├── components/
│   ├── layout/
│   ├── navigation/
│   ├── home/
│   ├── service/
│   ├── search/
│   ├── remote/
│   ├── faq/
│   └── ui/
│
├── data/
│   ├── services.ts
│   ├── categories.ts
│   └── faq.ts
│
├── lib/
│   ├── config.ts
│   ├── seo.ts
│   ├── search.ts
│   └── utils.ts
│
├── types/
│   └── index.ts
│
└── config/
    └── site.ts
```

The exact directory structure may evolve, but separation of:

- Content data
- Components
- Site configuration
- Utility logic

must remain clear.

---

# 23. CONTENT DATA MODEL

## 23.1 Service Type

Each service should be represented by structured data.

Minimum conceptual fields:

```text
id
slug
name
category
shortDescription
description
problemKeywords
useCases
requirements
processSteps
importantNotices
remoteAvailable
featured
seo
```

---

## 23.2 Category Type

Minimum fields:

```text
id
slug
name
description
icon
```

---

## 23.3 Site Configuration

Centralize:

```text
brandName
siteUrl
whatsappNumber
telegramUrl
supportEmail
socialLinks
remoteSoftwareLinks
```

No duplicated production contact information.

---

# 24. COMPONENT INVENTORY

## 24.1 Global Components

- SiteHeader
- MobileNavigation
- SiteFooter
- SearchInput
- Breadcrumbs
- ConsultationCTA
- SectionHeading

## 24.2 Service Components

- ServiceCard
- ServiceGrid
- ServiceHero
- ServiceOverview
- RequirementsList
- ProcessTimeline
- ImportantNotice
- ServiceContactCTA

## 24.3 Remote Components

- PreparationChecklist
- RemoteStep
- DownloadCard
- SecurityNotice

## 24.4 Search Components

- SearchDialog or SearchPage
- SearchResults
- EmptySearchState
- SearchSuggestion

## 24.5 FAQ Components

- FAQAccordion
- FAQCategory

---

# 25. PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS

The website must prioritize perceived and actual performance.

Requirements:

- Avoid unnecessary client components.
- Use optimized images.
- Avoid oversized assets.
- Avoid heavy animation libraries unless justified.
- Avoid loading scripts that are not required.
- Minimize layout shift.
- Keep interactions responsive.

The frontend must not sacrifice performance for decorative effects.

---

# 26. ERROR AND EMPTY STATES

Required states:

## Search No Results

```text
Tidak menemukan layanan yang sesuai.

Coba gunakan kata kunci lain atau hubungi CS untuk konsultasi.
```

## Missing Service

Use a proper 404 page.

## Broken or Missing Data

Do not render empty visual shells.

Provide a graceful fallback.

## External Contact Failure

If an external contact URL cannot be opened, the website should still expose alternative contact methods where possible.

---

# 27. TRUST AND SAFETY REQUIREMENTS

The product handles technical service discovery and may involve remote access.

The UI must prioritize customer awareness.

Required principles:

- Clearly identify official communication channels.
- Warn users not to grant remote access to unknown parties.
- Do not request unnecessary sensitive data.
- Do not imply guaranteed outcomes.
- Display relevant technical risks before execution-related actions.

---

# 28. FUTURE MARKETPLACE READINESS

The current frontend must not build full marketplace features.

However, data architecture should avoid assumptions that make future expansion difficult.

Future entities may include:

## Technician

```text
id
name
avatar
bio
specializations
services
rating
availability
```

## Customer

```text
id
profile
devices
orders
```

## Order

```text
id
service
customer
technician
status
payment
createdAt
```

These are future considerations only.

Do not implement UI for these entities unless actual product scope changes.

---

# 29. MVP DEFINITION

The MVP is complete when the following is functional and polished.

## Discovery

- Homepage
- Service categories
- Featured services
- Service listing
- Search

## Service Understanding

- Service detail pages
- Requirements
- Process explanation
- Important notices

## Conversion

- WhatsApp CTA
- Telegram CTA
- Contextual consultation messages

## Remote Preparation

- Remote guide
- AnyDesk official download destinations
- Preparation checklist
- Security notice

## Quality

- Responsive
- Accessible
- SEO-ready
- No fake data
- No dead CTA
- No unfinished placeholder content

---

# 30. ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA

## Homepage

PASS when:

- A new user understands the business purpose quickly.
- Search is discoverable.
- Categories are easy to scan.
- Consultation CTA is obvious.
- Mobile layout is usable.

## Services

PASS when:

- Every service has a stable URL.
- Service information comes from structured data.
- Each page has requirements and process information.
- Consultation CTA includes service context.

## Search

PASS when:

- Relevant static services can be found by name or keyword.
- Empty state is clear.
- Search does not require a backend.

## Remote Guide

PASS when:

- Customer understands what to prepare.
- Official remote software destination is clearly identified.
- Security guidance is present.
- User is not instructed to grant access blindly.

## SEO

PASS when:

- Service pages have unique metadata.
- Sitemap and robots configuration exist.
- Important content is crawlable.
- Semantic HTML is used.

---

# 31. IMPLEMENTATION RULES FOR CODING AGENT

The coding agent must follow these rules.

## Do

1. Read this PRD before implementation.
2. Build the frontend around reusable components.
3. Use structured static data.
4. Prioritize mobile experience.
5. Keep content architecture scalable.
6. Implement real navigation.
7. Implement real search against available static data.
8. Use centralized configuration.
9. Add SEO metadata.
10. Test responsive layouts.

## Do Not

1. Do not create fake backend features.
2. Do not invent ratings or testimonials.
3. Do not create fake payment flows.
4. Do not create fake order tracking.
5. Do not clone Shopee or Lazada.
6. Do not use excessive gradients.
7. Do not use random inconsistent spacing.
8. Do not place every element inside cards.
9. Do not hardcode contact information repeatedly.
10. Do not leave lorem ipsum or placeholder text in production pages.

---

# 32. UI QUALITY GATE

Before considering the implementation complete, verify:

## Visual

- Consistent spacing
- Consistent typography
- Consistent button hierarchy
- Consistent cards
- No excessive decoration

## Functional

- All navigation works
- All CTAs work
- Search works
- Mobile menu works
- External links are intentional

## Content

- No lorem ipsum
- No fake metrics
- No unsupported claims
- No duplicate content without purpose

## Technical

- TypeScript errors resolved
- Build passes
- Lint passes
- No obvious console errors
- Responsive behavior tested

---

